.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt MAN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm man
.Nd read a manual page
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar SECTION
.Ar NAME
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the manual page
.Ar NAME ,
from section
.Ar SECTION
when one is given and otherwise from the first of sections 1, 8, 2, 4, 5
and 7 that has it.
The Rach2 manual begins with
.Xr rach2 7 .
.Pp
The pages are preformatted text, gzipped, under
.Pa /usr/share/man/catN/NAME.N ,
rendered on the host by mandoc.
.Pa /usr/share/man/aliases
lists the names a page answers for besides its own, one
.Ql NAME SECTION TARGET
a line: every BusyBox applet is an alias of its family page, and every
member of a system-call family is an alias of the family's page.
.Nm
looks a name up as an alias first, then as a page.
.Pp
When standard output is a terminal, the page goes through the pager named
by
.Ev MANPAGER ,
then
.Ev PAGER ,
then BusyBox
.Xr less 1 ,
whose display width
.Ev COLUMNS
sets; the pages are not reformatted to it.
Otherwise the page goes to standard output as it is, so
.Ql man NAME > FILE
copies the page byte for byte.
.Pp
.Nm
is a shell script of this project.
BusyBox's man applet is not built: it finds a page only as a file, and a
file for each of the four hundred aliases would put that many nodes on
every root, which the RAM root and a KRFFS disk walk on every lookup.
.Pp
The manual sections are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 1
Programs.
.It 2
System calls.
.It 4
Devices.
.It 5
File formats.
.It 7
Overviews.
.It 8
Administration.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/share/man/catN/NAME.N
.It Pa /usr/share/man/catN/NAME.N
Installed preformatted pages.
.It Pa /usr/share/man/aliases
The names a page answers for.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is the pager's or 0, 1 when no page has the name, and 2
for a wrong argument count, which prints
.Ql usage: man [SECTION] NAME .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl man rach2
.Dl man 2 open
.Dl man ls
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr less 1 ,
.Xr rach2 7
